the genre of science fiction is vast  there are thousands and thousands of  books to be discovered and enjoyed and  there are also a fair bit of books that  are just uninspired or just downright  bad many of these books are derivative  and shallow and just don't offer  anything new to the genre I recently  completed the first book in the expanse  series Leviathan wakes I was amazed at  by how authors ty Franck and Daniel  Abraham were able to work within the  genre building on some older science  fiction concepts while offering a fresh  new lens through which to view them the  series follows human society in its  early stages of space colonization and I  can say with certainty that the first  book was a great introduction into its  complex and deeply thoughtful narrative  but this video isn't about the expanse  this video is about the novel that is  often cited as the best-selling science  fiction book in history dune dune is a  book for fans who have read a lot of the  genre and are now looking for a  challenge dune is not an easy read by  any stretch of the imagination but the  journey that Frank Herbert takes us on  through his series and the lessons that  we are taught along the way make the  story worthwhile in this video I will do  my best to list some of the top reasons  where the dune saga is actually worth  your time this video will contain  minimal spoilers I assure you  [Music]  the beauty of the writing the beautiful  language that Frank Herbert utilizes  throughout his series is one of the main  things that makes Dunes stand out  Herbert has a way of imbuing his words  with a kind of mysterious power that  makes you want to contemplate single  passages for hours on end  the poetic nature of Frank's writing  makes dune feel operatic and epic giving  the reader a sense of grandiosity it can  be somewhat challenging to decipher the  meaning behind the lyrical language of  dune especially for those that are used  to more straightforward styles of  writing for it Herbert actually  carefully designed the rhythm of dune to  influence the human mind  subconsciously this happens in many  poems the poem then develops a certain  fixed rhythm now by changing the  phraseology the placement of words you  can change that rhythm you can slow it  down you can speed it up well there is  an analogous thing in prose length of  sentence modifying clauses variety of  sentence structure all of these things  control the pace of controlled reading  Frank Herbert believed that even when  language was read silently it was still  received subconsciously as an oral  transmission Frank Herbert wrote doomed  the way he did purposely to control the  oral pacing of the narrative Herbert  described the pacing of dune as a  Collider rhythm starting slowly and  gradually building all the way up and  stopping suddenly at a non-breaking  point Frank Herbert intended the reader  to skid out of the first book trailing  parts of it with them learn the origin  of many science fiction tropes dune was  written in 1965 and has gone on to  influence the entire genre Frank Herbert  famously pointed out that there were 16  points of identity between dune and star  wars Frank Herbert believed that this  was in no way a coincidence at UCLA in  1985 during a talk Frank Herbert  calculated that it was a statistical  impossibility stating the odds of that  being a coincidence there aren't that  many stars in the  George Lucas no doubt borrowed many  elements from the doom saga but the main  themes of both series are actually quite  different there are many other works  that have borrowed elements from dune as  well the extremely expensive substance  the spice melange which had properties  which extended life for those who  ingested it likely inspired Polson  treatments from Dan Simmons Hyperion  cantos and almost certainly inspired the  substance known as booster spice from  Larry Nivens Ringworld series and the  list goes on and on Frank Herbert's  influence even extended out to the  fantasy genre with Robert Jordan likely  modeling the ice ax die on the Binney  jesuits sisterhood if you'd like to hear  more about this specific comparison  check out this video I did not too long  ago dune also inspired the graphic novel  the inkle written by Alejandro  Jodorowsky which itself went on to  inspire the movie The Fifth Element even  Shaun Murray creator of the exploratory  science fiction game no man's sky said  that dune was his favorite book ever and  had a giant influence on who he is as a  person no doubt dooms cultural impact  has been massive expansive imaginative  stories Frank Herbert believed that  stories that were remembered sparks  sparks in the minds of the reader though  a lot of things borrowed from dune there  really is no story quite like the one  portrayed in the dune saga the original  sagas narrative goes on for thousands of  years we see the Empire rise and fall  and rise again we see the culture of the  Imperium as it changes and evolves we  even see how their language evolves over  time Frank Herbert also filled his  universe with many different groups of  human beings each competing like the  Bene Gesserit sisterhood who desired a  specific kind of power they wished to  create the ultimate psychological weapon  or the play lacks ooh the technocratic  xenophobes on the Empire's edge who had  twisted ambitions of their own or the  Exynos the Masters of technological  innovation in the Imperium they  sometimes push the bounds of the ban of  thinking machines  and could very well invent catastrophe  for all the world building or more  accurately put a universe building in  the dune saga is absolutely phenomenal  lessons to be learned there are many  valuable things that one can take from  the dune saga let's look at how Frank  Herbert presents morality and ethics for  instance morality and ethics are two  words that are closely tied but there is  a very distinct difference morality  refers to a person's internal sense of  right and wrong  while ethics stem from a social system  ethics are rules governed by society at  the same time there are collective moral  norms take Furman culture for example  their moral norms are dictated by the  conditions of their environment moral  injunctions grew out of necessity ethics  are formed when a person recognizes the  consequences of moral law and modifies  it for example in the book dune frame  and morals dictate that a new leader  must call out the old one they expect  Paul to do this but Paul recognizes the  usefulness of the old leader and knows  that it would be a loss for them all  here  Paul modifies the frame and moral norm  using ethics and this is just one  example the dune Sava is filled with  these types of philosophical lessons  Frank Herbert's goal was not only to  entertain the reader but to make the  reader think about the meta message  beyond what is presented on the surface  I actually ended up writing a lot more  here about how Frank Herbert presents  the struggle between morality and ethics  but I decided that that needs its own  video there is so much to be said about  dune but obviously most of it can't be  said without spoilers as I have always  maintained dune is not an easy read but  it is a rewarding one if you want to  know more about the dune series click on  the dune playlist in the description of  this video thank you guys so much for  watching make sure you like and  subscribe if you enjoy the video and if  you want to support this channel click  the patreon link in the description also  check me out on Twitter and Facebook
 

